// Asking the collector to run.
//
// `heap.ft` and not `gc.ft`, which is a name no file may have: `gc` is a
// keyword, so no segment of an import's path can be one.
//
// Section 8 asked for two things about a `gc` and this is the second: "there
// is no spelling for when it runs". There is one now, and it is one routine --
// the collector's own decision about when to start a cycle is unchanged, and
// this is a program saying it knows something the collector does not.
//
// **When to write it, which is rarely.** The collector starts a cycle when the
// heap has grown past what the last one left plus a share of it
// (`runtime/src/gc.rs`), and that is the right answer for a program that
// allocates steadily. What it cannot know is a program that has just finished
// with a great deal at once -- a pass over a file, a request answered -- and is
// about to sit still. A program that knows that can say so here, and a program
// that does not should not.
//
// **What it promises.** That a whole cycle runs before it comes back: marking
// from the roots and sweeping what nothing reached. What it does not promise is
// that anything in particular is taken back. The roots are scanned
// conservatively (§8) -- a word on a stack is an address or a number with
// nothing to say which -- so a value nothing wants may still be held by a stale
// word, and no cycle will free it. That is the same fact that makes a `gc` of
// something with a release refused: a collector that cannot say what it took
// back cannot be given a job that has to happen.

// A whole cycle, now, on this thread. It comes back when the cycle is over.
%symbol("__rt_collect")
pub fn collect()
